    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Collagen for gut health rests on far thinner evidence than the marketing implies.</strong> One uncontrolled study in 14 women reported less bloating on 20,000mg a day, and the only randomised trial that measured gut barrier function found no effect at all. Collagen might help your gut; nothing published yet shows that it does.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One study, no placebo:</strong> 40 women recruited, 14 finished, 13 of those 14 reported fewer digestive symptoms on 20,000mg a day for eight weeks (<em>JMIR Formative Research</em>, 2022).</li>
        <li><strong>The controlled trial found nothing:</strong> 10,000mg a day for seven days changed neither the lactulose to rhamnose permeability ratio, inflammation nor gut symptoms in a randomised crossover trial of 20 adults (<em>European Journal of Nutrition</em>, 2023).</li>
        <li><strong>"Leaky gut" is not a diagnosis:</strong> the Cleveland Clinic calls leaky gut syndrome a hypothetical condition, not currently recognised as a medical diagnosis, with no standard test for permeability in patients.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> Collabloom's 8,000mg is 40% of the dose in the one study reporting a digestive benefit, and we have no gut data of our own.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

