      <h2 id="direct-evidence">Is collagen good for gut health?</h2>
      <p>Nobody knows, and the evidence is weaker than almost every page selling it suggests. Two human studies have looked at collagen peptides and the digestive tract: the one reporting a benefit had no control group, and the randomised controlled one found nothing.</p>
      <p>The study everyone cites is by Abrahams, O'Grady and Prawitt in <em>JMIR Formative Research</em>, 2022. Participants tracked symptoms in an app for two weeks, then took 20,000mg of collagen peptides a day, split into two doses, for eight weeks. Among those who finished, 13 of 14 reported fewer digestive symptoms including bloating, and the authors concluded the supplement <em>may</em> reduce bloating in otherwise healthy female adults.</p>
      <p>Three things matter more than that headline. There was no placebo arm, so there is nothing to compare the improvement against. Forty people were recruited and 14 completed, a 65% dropout. And the collagen tested was Peptan, made by Rousselot, with a Rousselot affiliation among the authors.</p>
      <p>Without a control group, a symptom diary kept by volunteers who wanted less bloating cannot separate the supplement from the ordinary ebb and flow of gut symptoms, or from the act of paying attention. The authors were candid, noting that reports of the benefits of collagen in digestive health are currently lacking.</p>

