      <h2 id="controlled-trial">What did the controlled trial find?</h2>
      <p>Nothing, on every outcome it was built to detect. This is the study the seller pages leave out.</p>
      <p>Taylor and colleagues, writing in the <em>European Journal of Nutrition</em> in 2023, ran a randomised crossover trial in 20 volunteers. Each took 10,000mg of collagen peptides a day or a placebo for seven days, then ran for 70 minutes at 70 to 90% of VO<sub>2</sub>max — a protocol used because hard endurance exercise measurably increases gut permeability, one of the few ways to test a gut barrier intervention in healthy people.</p>
      <p>Collagen did not change the lactulose to rhamnose permeability ratio, the inflammatory markers, or reported gut symptoms. One measure did differ: serum LPS, a marker of bacterial endotoxin in the blood, stayed lower two hours after exercise on collagen than on placebo. The authors called that a possible attenuation worth investigating, not a result to act on.</p>

