      <h2 id="leaky-gut">Is "leaky gut" a real condition?</h2>
      <p>Increased intestinal permeability is real and measurable in a laboratory. "Leaky gut syndrome", as a diagnosis you can be given and treated for, is not.</p>
      <p>The Cleveland Clinic describes it as a hypothetical condition, not currently recognised as a medical diagnosis, and says there is no standard test to measure intestinal permeability directly in patients. UPMC makes the same point from the testing side: normal values are not well defined, protocols are not standardised, and many available tests have not been fully validated.</p>
      <p>The deeper problem is direction: where raised permeability shows up, it is unresolved whether it causes the disease or results from it. That is why no treatment, supplement or drug, has been shown to improve a clinical outcome by lowering permeability. So if you buy a leaky gut test and it reads abnormal, there is no validated range to read it against and no action that follows. That test is the clearest avoidable cost here.</p>

