      <h2 id="mechanism">Why does everyone say collagen repairs the gut lining?</h2>
      <p>Because of its amino acids, and because of experiments in cells and animals. Not because of trials in people.</p>
      <p>Glycine makes up roughly a third of collagen's amino acid residues, with proline and hydroxyproline together about another fifth to a quarter. Glycine does measurable things to the intestinal barrier in the laboratory. In pig intestinal cells, physiological glycine concentrations raised barrier resistance, lowered paracellular permeability and held the junction proteins claudin-7 and ZO-3 in place, though a higher concentration reduced ZO-1. In mice on a high-fat diet, glycine in drinking water altered tight-junction proteins in the jejunum and colon. In rats given a pesticide, it reduced intestinal permeability.</p>
      <p>That is cells, pigs, mice and rats, mostly reading out genes rather than symptoms, and not even uniform: in broiler chickens under heat stress, jejunal tight-junction genes did not differ between diets. A mechanism that works in a dish is a reason to run a trial, not to make a claim.</p>
      <p>There is also something collagen is not. It is an incomplete protein, containing no tryptophan, which is why a 2019 analysis in <em>Nutrients</em> set a ceiling of about 36% of daily protein coming from collagen peptides while still meeting indispensable amino acid needs. If your symptoms are really about <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">not eating enough protein overall</a>, collagen is the wrong protein to fix them with. How collagen peptides are absorbed is a separate and better-answered question.</p>

      <h2 id="bloating-india">What is actually causing your bloating?</h2>
      <p>In India the likeliest answer is dairy, and unlike leaky gut it is testable. Work through this list first.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Lactose.</strong> An older multicentre Indian study reported lactose intolerance in 27.4% of people in north India and 66.6% in southern India, and later breath-testing and genetic work also found malabsorption more common in the south. A two-week trial without milk, curd or paneer answers this for free.</li>
        <li><strong>Irritable bowel syndrome.</strong> Among 100 adults with Rome IV-defined IBS at a centre in Gurugram, 38% also had lactose intolerance, so the two overlap and treating one may not settle the other.</li>
        <li><strong>Specific triggers, not a blanket diet.</strong> A study of self-reported food intolerances in an Indian population concluded that individualisation is needed rather than a universal low-FODMAP diet. Cutting whole food groups on principle costs more nutrition than it buys comfort.</li>
        <li><strong>Coeliac disease.</strong> Underdiagnosed, and a blood test. Rule it out before any experiment with wheat, because the test is unreliable once you have stopped eating gluten.</li>
        <li><strong>How you eat.</strong> Eating speed, aerated drinks, very large late dinners and long gaps between meals all produce bloating no supplement will touch.</li>
        <li><strong>The supplement itself.</strong> Fullness, heaviness and loose stools are among the <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">reported side effects of collagen supplements</a>. If symptoms started when the supplement did, stop it for two weeks and see.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="gut-skin">Does your gut affect your skin?</h2>
      <p>There is a genuine research field here, the gut–skin axis, and it is not about collagen.</p>
      <p>Reviews link gut microbial imbalance to acne, psoriasis and atopic dermatitis, and the interventions being studied are probiotics and prebiotics. A placebo-controlled study of spore-based probiotics found gut microbial profiles differed between people with and without acne, and that supplementation shifted the microbiome and short-chain fatty acids; its authors called that preliminary. For eczema, a Cochrane review found only modest improvement with high variability between trials.</p>
      <p>Those are living organisms swallowed to change a microbial community. Collagen is a hydrolysed protein and is neither a probiotic nor a prebiotic. The gut–skin axis is not a back door through which a collagen shot clears your skin: <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen's skin evidence</a> stands on its own.</p>

      <h2 id="where-it-fits">Where a collagen shot honestly fits</h2>
      <p>Not as a gut treatment, and we will not pretend otherwise on a page about gut health.</p>
      <p>Collabloom is a 30ml daily shot carrying 8,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen, 250mg of vitamin C from amla, 250mg of glutathione, 100mg of hyaluronic acid, 30mg of oral ceramides and 240mg of Polypodium leucotomos, at 35 kcal and sugar free. Those doses were chosen against skin, hair and nail evidence, because that is where the ingredients have been measured.</p>
      <p>Here is the arithmetic we would rather you got from us. The only study reporting a digestive benefit used 20,000mg a day. Collabloom provides 8,000mg, 40% of it — and since that study had no placebo group, even its 20,000mg is not a target worth chasing. <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">The doses the trials actually used</a> differ by outcome, and for the gut there is no established dose at all.</p>
      <p>If you take Collabloom and your digestion feels better, we would not argue with your experience. It is not something we can claim or measure, and a collagen shot is not a treatment for any digestive condition.</p>

