      <h2 id="doctor">When to see a doctor instead</h2>
      <p>Bloating is usually benign, but some patterns need a doctor rather than a supplement shelf. See one promptly if your bloating is new, persistent beyond two to three weeks or steadily worsening, and especially if any of these are present:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Unintentional weight loss, especially more than 5 to 10% of body weight over six months</li>
        <li>Blood in the stool or rectal bleeding</li>
        <li>Iron-deficiency anaemia with no obvious cause, or unexplained fatigue and pallor</li>
        <li>Persistent vomiting, severe abdominal pain or fever</li>
        <li>New constipation starting after the age of 50</li>
        <li>Diarrhoea that wakes you from sleep</li>
        <li>A family history of colorectal cancer, coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Bloating with an inability to pass gas or stool is an emergency, not a clinic appointment. These thresholds come from gastroenterology clinic guidance rather than one formal guideline and the numbers differ between sources, so treat the pattern as the signal, not the week count.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen good for gut health?</summary>
          <p>There is no good evidence that it is. The only study reporting a digestive benefit was uncontrolled, with 14 completers out of 40 recruited on 20,000mg a day. The one randomised controlled trial, which measured gut permeability, found no effect.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help bloating?</summary>
          <p>It has not been shown to. The 2022 study in which 13 of 14 women reported less bloating had no placebo group, so the improvement cannot be credited to the collagen. In India, a two-week trial without lactose is a better first move.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen heal leaky gut?</summary>
          <p>No, and leaky gut syndrome is not a recognised medical diagnosis. The Cleveland Clinic calls it a hypothetical condition with no standard test for permeability in patients. The one randomised trial that measured a permeability ratio found collagen did not change it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen cause bloating or constipation?</summary>
          <p>Some people report fullness, heaviness or loose stools on collagen. If your symptoms began when the supplement did, stop it for two weeks and see whether they settle. No trial has compared a liquid shot against a powder for digestive comfort.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen would I need for gut health?</summary>
          <p>There is no established gut dose, because no controlled trial has shown a gut benefit at any dose. The uncontrolled 2022 study used 20,000mg a day; the randomised trial that found nothing used 10,000mg. Collabloom provides 8,000mg per 30ml shot.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is a probiotic better than collagen for gut health?</summary>
          <p>For gut symptoms, probiotics have far more trial evidence, though results vary by strain and the effects are often modest. Collagen is a hydrolysed protein, neither a probiotic nor a prebiotic. Neither replaces working out what is triggering your symptoms.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take a collagen shot if I have IBS?</summary>
          <p>Ask the doctor managing your IBS, because triggers differ between people. Collabloom is a 30ml sugar-free shot sweetened with stevia, and both are worth mentioning when you ask. It is not a treatment for IBS.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

